{"name":"White Heron","hex":"#e7e1d7","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"white","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Bright and colourless; reflecting equal quantities of all frequencies of visible light.","Of or relating to Europeans or those of European descent, regardless if their skin has cool or warm undertones."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of snow or milk; the color of light containing equal amounts of all visible wavelengths.","A person of European descent with light-colored skin."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/white"},{"term":"heron","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any long-legged, long-necked wading bird of the family Ardeidae."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/heron"}],"entries":[{"title":"Great egret","blocks":[{"text":"The great egret, also known as the common egret, large egret, great white egret, or great white heron, is a large, widely distributed egret. The four subspecies are found in Asia, Africa, the Americas, and southern Europe. Recently, it has also been spreading to more northern areas of Europe. Distributed across most of the tropical and warmer temperate regions of the world, it builds tree nests in colonies close to water."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/Great_Egret_%28Ardea_alba%29_in_Breeding_Plumage%2C_Cape_May_County%2C_New_Jersey%2C_USA_%28cropped%29.png/330px-Great_Egret_%28Ardea_alba%29_in_Breeding_Plumage%2C_Cape_May_County%2C_New_Jersey%2C_USA_%28cropped%29.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Great_egret","wikidata":"Q130730"},{"title":"Eastern great egret","blocks":[{"text":"The eastern great egret is a species of heron from the genus Ardea, usually considered a subspecies of the great egret. In New Zealand it is known as the white heron or by its Māori name kōtuku. It was first described by British ornithologist John Edward Gray in 1831."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/51/Kotuku_%C2%B7_Rina_Sjardin-Thompson_WCSW.jpg/330px-Kotuku_%C2%B7_Rina_Sjardin-Thompson_WCSW.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eastern_great_egret","wikidata":"Q1207168"},{"title":"Heron","blocks":[{"text":"Herons are long-legged, long-necked, freshwater and coastal birds in the family Ardeidae, with 75 recognised species, some of which are referred to as egrets or bitterns rather than herons. Members of the genus Botaurus are referred to as bitterns, and, together with the zigzag heron, or zigzag bittern, in the monotypic genus Zebrilus, form a monophyletic group, Botaurinae, within the Ardeidae family. Egrets do not form a biologically distinct group from herons, and tend to be named differently because they are mainly white or have decorative plumes in breeding plumage. Herons, by evolutionary adaptation, have long beaks."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/Ardeidae-01.jpg/330px-Ardeidae-01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Heron","wikidata":"Q18789"},{"title":"White","blocks":[{"text":"White is the lightest color and is achromatic. It is the color of objects such as snow, chalk, and milk, and is the opposite of black. White objects fully reflect and scatter all the visible wavelengths of light. White on television and computer screens is created by a mixture of red, blue, and green light. The color white can be given with white pigments, especially titanium dioxide."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg/330px-Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White","wikidata":"Q23444"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thu